"hadiths[15]{hadithnumber,text}:"
148901,"Malik related to me from Nafi that Abdullah ibn Umar said, ""Whoever does not decide firmly to fast before dawn should not fast."""
148902,"Malik related to me from Nafi that Abdullah ibn Umar said, ""The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, made zakat al-fitr obligatory,"
149001,"Malik related to me that he had heard that Urwa ibn az-Zubayr and Sulayman ibn Yasar said, ""The mukatab is a slave as long as any of his kitaba remains to be paid."" Malik said, ""This is my opinion as well."" Malik said, ""If a mukatab dies and leaves more property than what remains to be paid of his kitaba and he has children who were born during the time of his kitaba or whose kitaba has been written as well, they inherit any property that remains after the kitaba has been paid"
149002,"Malik related to me that he had heard that Urwa ibn az-Zubayr and Sulayman ibn Yasar said, ""The mukatab is a slave as long as any of his kitaba remains to be paid."" Malik said, ""This is my opinion as well."" Malik said, ""If a mukatab dies and leaves more property than what remains to be paid of his kitaba and he has children who were born during the time of his kitaba or whose kitaba has been written as well, they inherit any property that remains after the kitaba has been paid"
149101,Malik related to me from Humayd ibn Qays al-Makki that a son of al-Mutawakkil had a mukatab who died at Makka and left (enough to pay) the rest of his kitaba and he owed some debts to people. He also left a daughter. The governor of Makka was not certain about how to judge in the case
149102,Malik related to me from Humayd ibn Qays al-Makki that a son of al-Mutawakkil had a mukatab who died at Makka and left (enough to pay) the rest of his kitaba and he owed some debts to people. He also left a daughter. The governor of Makka was not certain about how to judge in the case
1492,"[1492] Yahya narrated to me, on the authority of Malik, that he had heard that Urwah ibn al-Zubair used to say about the son of the cursed man and the son of Adultery: If he dies, his mother inherits his rights according to the Book of God Almighty, and his brothers have his rights to his mother, and he inherits The survivor is his mother’s slave, if she is your slave, and if she is an Arab, she inherits her rights, and his brothers inherit from his mother. Their rights, and what remained for the Muslims was a foothold in front of Malik Hadith: 1492 Arabic Hadith:"
149301,"Yahya said: Malik said from Muhammad ibn Umara from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm that Uthman ibn Affan said: ""When boundaries fall in the land, there is no pre-emption in it, nor in a well, nor in a palm pollinator."" Malik said: ""And on this is the ruling among us."" Malik said: ""And there is no pre-emption in a road, whether division is valid in it or not."" Malik said: ""And the ruling among us is that there is no pre-emption in a house courtyard, whether division is valid in it or not."" Malik said: ""Concerning a man who buys a share of shared land on condition that he has the option in it, and the seller's partners want to take what their partner sold by pre-emption before the buyer chooses: that is not for them until the buyer takes and the sale is established for him. When the sale becomes binding for him, they have pre-emption."" And Malik said: ""Concerning the man who buys land, it remains in his hands for a while, then a man comes and establishes a right in it by inheritance: he has pre-emption if his right is established. And whatever the land produces of produce is for the first buyer until the day the other's right is established, because he had guaranteed it - if what was in it of plantings perished or a flood took it."" He said: ""If the time is prolonged, or witnesses perish, or the seller or buyer dies, or both are alive but the origin of the sale and purchase is forgotten due to the length of time, then pre-emption is cut off, and he takes his established right. But if his matter is otherwise than this, in recent and near time, and he sees that the seller concealed the price and hid it to cut off the pre-emptor's right by that, the land is valued at what is seen to be its price, so its price becomes that. Then what increased in the land of building, planting, or development is looked at, and it is on the basis of what is on one who bought land for a known price, then built and planted in it, then the pre-emptor took it after that."" Malik said: ""And pre-emption is established in the dead's wealth as it is in the living's wealth. If the dead's heirs fear the dead's wealth will be broken up by division, they divide it then sell it; there is no pre-emption on them in it."" Malik said: ""And there is no pre-emption among us in a slave, nor slave girl, nor camel, nor cow, nor sheep, nor in anything of livestock, nor in a garment, nor in a well that has no white land. Pre-emption is only in what is divisible and in which boundaries fall, of land. As for what division is not valid in, there is no pre-emption in it."" Malik said: ""And whoever buys land in which there is pre-emption for people present, let him refer them to the ruler. Either they establish their right, or the ruler hands it to him. If he leaves them and does not refer their matter to the ruler, and they knew of his purchase, then they left that until the time was prolonged, then they came demanding their pre-emption: I do not see that for them."""""""
149302,"[1493] Malik said: ""And it reached me from Sulayman ibn Yasar the like of that. Malik said: 'And on that I found the people of knowledge in our land.'"" Muwatta of Imam Malik, Hadith: 1493, Arabic Hadith."
149401,"Yahya said: Malik said from Dawud ibn al-Husayn that he heard Abu Ghatafan ibn Tarif al-Murri say: ""Zayd ibn Thabit al-Ansari and Ibn Muti' disputed over a house between them to Marwan ibn al-Hakam, who was amir over Medina. Marwan judged against Zayd ibn Thabit with the oath on the minbar. Zayd ibn Thabit said: 'I will swear in my place.' Marwan said: 'No, by Allah, only at the place where rights are cut off (i.e., decided).' So Zayd ibn Thabit swore that his right was truly a right. And he refused to swear on the minbar."" He said: ""Marwan ibn al-Hakam wondered at that."" Malik said: ""I do not see that anyone should be made to swear on the minbar for less than a quarter dinar, and that is three dirhams."""""""
149402,"[1494] Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from his father: ""That in the book which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote for Amr ibn Hazm concerning blood-money (diya): 'In the life (nafs) is one hundred camels. In the nose, if it is cut off completely, one hundred camels. In the brain wound (ma'muma), a third of the blood-money. In the belly wound (ja'ifa), the like of it. In the eye, fifty. In the hand, fifty. In the foot, fifty. In each finger of those there, ten camels. In the tooth, five. In the head wound that lays bare the bone (muḍiḥa), five.'"" Muwatta of Imam Malik, Hadith: 1494, Arabic Hadith."
149501,"Malik related to me that he heard Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman and others mention that al-Furafisa ibn Umar al-Hanafi had a mukatab who offered to pay him all of his kitaba that he owed. Al-Furafisa refused to accept it and the mukatab went to Marwan ibn al-Hakam who was the amir of Madina and brought up the matter. Marwan summoned al-Furafisa and told him to accept. He refused. Marwan then ordered that the payment be taken from the mukatab and placed in the treasury. He said to the mukatab \\""Go"""""""
149502,"Malik related to me that he heard Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman and others mention that al-Furafisa ibn Umar al-Hanafi had a mukatab who offered to pay him all of his kitaba that he owed. Al-Furafisa refused to accept it and the mukatab went to Marwan ibn al-Hakam who was the amir of Madina and brought up the matter. Marwan summoned al-Furafisa and told him to accept. He refused. Marwan then ordered that the payment be taken from the mukatab and placed in the treasury. He said to the mukatab \\""Go"""""""
149601,"Malik related to me that he had heard that Said ibn al-Musayyab was asked about a mukatab who was shared between two men. One of them freed his portion and then the mukatab died and left a lot of money. Said replied, ""The one who kept his kitaba is paid what remains due to him, and then they divide what is left between them both equally."" Malik said, ""When a mukatab who fulfils his kitaba and becomes free dies, he is inherited from by the people who wrote his kitaba and their children and paternal relations - whoever is most deserving."" He said, ""This is also for whoever is set free when he dies after being set free - his inheritance is for the nearest people to him of children or paternal relations who inherit by means of the wala'."" Malik said, ""Brothers, written together in the same kitaba, are in the same position as children to each other when none of them have children written in the kitaba or born in the kitaba. When one of them dies and leaves property, he pays for them all that is against them of their kitaba and sets them free. The money left over after that goes to his children rather than his brothers"
149602,"Malik related to me that he had heard that Said ibn al-Musayyab was asked about a mukatab who was shared between two men. One of them freed his portion and then the mukatab died and left a lot of money. Said replied, ""The one who kept his kitaba is paid what remains due to him, and then they divide what is left between them both equally."" Malik said, ""When a mukatab who fulfils his kitaba and becomes free dies, he is inherited from by the people who wrote his kitaba and their children and paternal relations - whoever is most deserving."" He said, ""This is also for whoever is set free when he dies after being set free - his inheritance is for the nearest people to him of children or paternal relations who inherit by means of the wala'."" Malik said, ""Brothers, written together in the same kitaba, are in the same position as children to each other when none of them have children written in the kitaba or born in the kitaba. When one of them dies and leaves property, he pays for them all that is against them of their kitaba and sets them free. The money left over after that goes to his children rather than his brothers"
